				<div id="bodyContent" class="vector-body" aria-labelledby="firstHeading" data-mw-ve-target-container>
					<div class="vector-body-before-content">
							<div class="mw-indicators">
		</div>

						<div id="siteSub" class="noprint">From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia</div>
					</div>
					<div id="contentSub"><div id="mw-content-subtitle"></div></div>
					
					
					<div id="mw-content-text" class="mw-body-content"><div class="mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang="en" dir="ltr" data-mw-parsoid-version="0.24.0.0-alpha21" data-mw-html-version="2.8.0"><section data-mw-section-id="0" id="mwAQ"><div class="shortdescription nomobile noexcerpt noprint searchaux" style="display:none" about="#mwt1" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Short description","href":"./Template:Short_description"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Aspect of ancient Jewish art"}},"i":0}}]}'>Aspect of ancient Jewish art</div><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_with_short_description"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Short_description_matches_Wikidata"/></span>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAw"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Beit_alfa01.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBA"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Beit_alfa01.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Beit_alfa01.jpg/250px-Beit_alfa01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Beit_alfa01.jpg/500px-Beit_alfa01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="792" data-file-height="886" data-file-type="bitmap" height="280" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBQ"/></a><figcaption id="mwBg">Helios-zodiac cycle, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beth_Alpha" title="Beth Alpha" id="mwBw">Beth Alpha</a> Synagogue</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwCA">At least eight synagogues from ancient Israel (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Palestine" title="Roman Palestine" id="mwCQ">Roman Palestine</a>), in the 4th–6th centuries CE during the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byzantine_period" title="Byzantine period" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCg">Byzantine period</a>, contained <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zodiac" title="Zodiac" id="mwCw">zodiac</a> mosaics. As a typical arrangement, the mosaic floors of such synagogues consisted of three panels, the central one containing the Jewish zodiac.<sup about="#mwt2" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHachlili197761_1-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHachlili197761"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHachlili197761-1"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn ","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":" Hachlili "},"2":{"wt":" 1977 "},"p":{"wt":"61"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHachlili197761-1" id="mwDA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDg">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" id="mwEA" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwEQ" aria-labelledby="Inventory"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Inventory">Inventory</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Zodiac_mosaics_in_ancient_synagogues&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1" title="Edit section: Inventory"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwEg">The examples cited by Hachlili in 1977 are the synagogues at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hammat_Tiberias#"Synagogue_of_Severus"' title="Hammat Tiberias" id="mwEw">Hammat Tiberias</a> (4th century), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Husaifa" title="Husaifa" class="mw-redirect" id="mwFA">Husaifa</a> (5th century), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naaran#Synagogue" title="Naaran" id="mwFQ">Na'aran</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beth_Alpha" title="Beth Alpha" id="mwFg">Beth Alpha</a> (6th century).<sup about="#mwt5" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHachlili197761_1-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHachlili197761"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHachlili197761-1"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn ","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":" Hachlili "},"2":{"wt":" 1977 "},"p":{"wt":"61"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHachlili197761-1" id="mwFw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwGA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwGQ">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwGg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt10" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-RH02_2-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"RH02"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-RH02-2"}}'><a href="#cite_note-RH02-2" id="mwGw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwHA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHQ">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHg">]</span></span></a></sup> The large synagogue of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sepphoris" title="Sepphoris" id="mwHw">Sepphoris</a> (5th–6th century), more recently discovered, has a different panel scheme; the one at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susiya" title="Susiya" class="mw-redirect" id="mwIA">Susiya</a> probably had a zodiac mosaic in the 6th century, which was later replaced by a non-figurative pattern; at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/En_Gedi" title="En Gedi" class="mw-redirect" id="mwIQ">En Gedi</a> there is an inscription with the names of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zodiac_signs" title="Zodiac signs" class="mw-redirect" id="mwIg">zodiac signs</a> and the corresponding <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hebrew_calendar" title="Hebrew calendar" id="mwIw">Jewish months</a>, but no imagery; at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meroth" title="Meroth" class="mw-redirect" id="mwJA">Meroth</a> archaeologists found three slabs containing zodiac signs; the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yafi'a" title="Yafi'a" class="mw-redirect" id="mwJQ">Yafi'a</a> synagogue, with a different layout and whose twelve medallions set between two circles are lost except for one plus a second depicting the zodiac.<sup about="#mwt11" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-RH02_2-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"RH02"}}'><a href="#cite_note-RH02-2" id="mwJg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwJw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKA">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The 5th-century <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huqoq#Ancient_synagogue" title="Huqoq" id="mwKg">Huqoq synagogue's</a> mosaic floor also contains a depiction of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helios" title="Helios" id="mwKw">Helios</a>, the zodiac signs and personifications of the four seasons, next to images unique to this site.<sup about="#mwt12" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-3"}}'><a href="#cite_note-3" id="mwLA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwLQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLg">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLw">]</span></span></a></sup> The Huqoq design only resembles the one at Yafi'a, with Helios placed in a central medallion, which is surrounded by roundels interlaced with each other.<sup about="#mwt13" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Influ_4-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Influ"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Influ-4"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Influ-4" id="mwMA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwMQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMg">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMw">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamaam,_Israel#Ancient_synagogue" title="Hamaam, Israel" class="mw-redirect" id="mwNA">Wadi Hamam synagogue</a>, not far from Huqoq, was very similar in its architectural plan and surviving mosaics, which makes researchers believe that it probably had its own prominent Helios-and-zodiac depiction, but the central part of the nave's mosaic floor is missing.<sup about="#mwt14" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Influ_4-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Influ"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Influ-4" id="mwNQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwNg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNw">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwOA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwOQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_Floor_in_Synagogue_at_Hammat_Tiberias_02.JPG" class="mw-file-description" id="mwOg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_Floor_in_Synagogue_at_Hammat_Tiberias_02.JPG" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Mosaic_Floor_in_Synagogue_at_Hammat_Tiberias_02.JPG/250px-Mosaic_Floor_in_Synagogue_at_Hammat_Tiberias_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Mosaic_Floor_in_Synagogue_at_Hammat_Tiberias_02.JPG/500px-Mosaic_Floor_in_Synagogue_at_Hammat_Tiberias_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1024" data-file-height="768" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwOw"/></a><figcaption id="mwPA">Helios and zodiac, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hammat_Tiberias" title="Hammat Tiberias" id="mwPQ">Hammat Tiberias</a> synagogue</figcaption></figure>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwPg" aria-labelledby="Analysis"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Analysis">Analysis</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Zodiac_mosaics_in_ancient_synagogues&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2" title="Edit section: Analysis"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<section data-mw-section-id="3" id="mwPw" aria-labelledby="Patterns,_aesthetics"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Patterns,_aesthetics"><span id="Patterns.2C_aesthetics" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Patterns, aesthetics</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Zodiac_mosaics_in_ancient_synagogues&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3" title="Edit section: Patterns, aesthetics"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwQA">Some of the buildings' art was remarkably well-preserved, giving a window into the specifics of partially-lost traditions. The craftsmanship of these as well as of other unexpected historical Jewish art has been previously called "touchingly naive," "untutored," and even "primitive," but these dismissals are now outdated.<sup about="#mwt17" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-LCh_5-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"LCh"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-LCh-5"}}'><a href="#cite_note-LCh-5" id="mwQQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwQg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQw">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwRQ">In 1993, the most elaborate mosaic yet was <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tzippori_Synagogue" title="Tzippori Synagogue" id="mwRg">found</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sepphoris" title="Sepphoris" id="mwRw">Sepphoris</a>. It has some uniques: scenes of the accessories and sacrifices of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_in_Jerusalem" title="Temple in Jerusalem" id="mwSA">Temple</a> and a scene of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham#Three_visitors" title="Abraham" id="mwSQ">angels visiting Abraham and Sarah</a>. "Elegant indeed... we find <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scorpio_(astrology)" title="Scorpio (astrology)" id="mwSg">Scorpio</a> (עקרב) together with its Hebrew month <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heshvan" title="Heshvan" class="mw-redirect" id="mwSw">Heshvan</a> (חשון), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sagittarius_(astrology)" title="Sagittarius (astrology)" id="mwTA">Sagittarius</a> (קשת) together with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kislev" title="Kislev" id="mwTQ">Kislev</a> (כסלו)".<sup about="#mwt20" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-BASh687_6-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"BASh687"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-BASh687-6"}}'><a href="#cite_note-BASh687-6" id="mwTg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwTw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUA">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwUg">Hachlili says Jewish communities "always used the same scheme for their floors."<sup about="#mwt21" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHachlili197761–77_7-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHachlili197761–77"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHachlili197761–77-7"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn ","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":" Hachlili "},"2":{"wt":" 1977 "},"pp":{"wt":"61–77"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHachlili197761–77-7" id="mwUw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwVA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVQ">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVg">]</span></span></a></sup> There are four figures (seasons) on the outer corners. Within is a roundel with twelve depictions. In the bullseye is a sun god with the appropriate horses-chariots imagery.</p>

<p id="mwVw">The Beth Alpha example has been called  one of Israel's great artistic treasures,<sup about="#mwt26" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Frommers_r949_8-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Frommers r949"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Frommers_r949-8"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Frommers_r949-8" id="mwWA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwWQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWg">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<blockquote id="mwXA">
<p id="mwXQ">so filled with feeling and so packed with information for scholars to study. It revolutionized ideas about ancient Jewish attitudes toward representational art, which many previously believed had been nonexistent.</p>
</blockquote>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mwXg" aria-labelledby="Religious_symbolism"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Religious_symbolism">Religious symbolism</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Zodiac_mosaics_in_ancient_synagogues&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4" title="Edit section: Religious symbolism"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwXw">Lucille A. Roussin sees the sun deity depicted in the mosaics as continuation of an ancient faith in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angels_in_Judaism" title="Angels in Judaism" id="mwYA">angels</a> seen as entities interposed between the highest, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jewish_cosmology#firmament" title="Jewish cosmology" id="mwYQ">seventh firmament</a> understood as the seat of God, and the earthly realm.<sup about="#mwt27" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-LAR_9-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"LAR"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-LAR-9"}}'><a href="#cite_note-LAR-9" id="mwYg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwYw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwZA">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwZQ">]</span></span></a></sup> As to the zodiac, she quotes <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josephus" title="Josephus" id="mwZg">Josephus</a>, who states not only that among the twelve stones of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Priest_of_Israel" title="High Priest of Israel" id="mwZw">high priest</a>'s <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Priestly_breastplate" title="Priestly breastplate" id="mwaA">breastplate</a> "[s]un and moon are indicated by the two sardonyxes", but also that "whether one would prefer to read in them the months or the constellations of like number, which the Greeks call the circle of the zodiac, he will not mistake the lawgiver's intention."<sup about="#mwt28" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-LAR_9-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"LAR"}}'><a href="#cite_note-LAR-9" id="mwaQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwag"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwaw">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwbA">]</span></span></a></sup> Note that the common rabbinic interpretation of the twelve stones is that they represent the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve_tribes_of_Israel" title="Twelve tribes of Israel" class="mw-redirect" id="mwbQ">twelve tribes of Israel</a>.<sup about="#mwt29" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-YA_10-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"YA"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-YA-10"}}'><a href="#cite_note-YA-10" id="mwbg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwbw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcA">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwcg"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jodi_Magness" title="Jodi Magness" id="mwcw">Jodi Magness</a> argues the sun god is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helios" title="Helios" id="mwdA">Helios</a> and to be identified with the angel <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metatron" title="Metatron" id="mwdQ">Metatron</a>. <sup about="#mwt30" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMagness20051–52_11-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMagness20051–52"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMagness20051–52-11"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn ","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":" Magness "},"2":{"wt":" 2005 "},"pp":{"wt":"1–52"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMagness20051–52-11" id="mwdg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwdw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mweA">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mweQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<ul class="gallery mw-gallery-traditional" typeof="mw:Extension/gallery" about="#mwt33" id="mweg" data-mw='{"name":"gallery","attrs":{},"body":{}}'>
<li class="gallerybox" style="width: 155px;" id="mwew"><div class="thumb" style="width: 150px; height: 150px;" id="mwfA"><span typeof="mw:File" id="mwfQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:ZodiacMosaicTzippori.jpg" class="mw-file-description" title="Helios and zodiac, Tzippori Synagogue" id="mwfg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:ZodiacMosaicTzippori.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/ZodiacMosaicTzippori.jpg/120px-ZodiacMosaicTzippori.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/ZodiacMosaicTzippori.jpg/250px-ZodiacMosaicTzippori.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Helios and zodiac, Tzippori Synagogue" data-file-width="1251" data-file-height="886" data-file-type="bitmap" height="85" width="120" class="mw-file-element" id="mwfw"/></a></span></div><div class="gallerytext" id="mwgA">Helios and zodiac, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tzippori_Synagogue" title="Tzippori Synagogue" id="mwgQ">Tzippori Synagogue</a></div></li>
<li class="gallerybox" style="width: 155px;" id="mwgg"><div class="thumb" style="width: 150px; height: 150px;" id="mwgw"><span typeof="mw:File" id="mwhA"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_Tribes.jpg" class="mw-file-description" title="The Twelve Tribes of Israel, modern synagogue mosaic" id="mwhQ"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_Tribes.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Mosaic_Tribes.jpg/120px-Mosaic_Tribes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Mosaic_Tribes.jpg/250px-Mosaic_Tribes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="The Twelve Tribes of Israel, modern synagogue mosaic" data-file-width="696" data-file-height="689" data-file-type="bitmap" height="119" width="120" class="mw-file-element" id="mwhg"/></a></span></div><div class="gallerytext" id="mwhw">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve_Tribes" title="Twelve Tribes" class="mw-redirect" id="mwiA">Twelve Tribes</a> of Israel, modern synagogue mosaic</div></li>
</ul>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="5" id="mwiQ" aria-labelledby="Similar_examples"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Similar_examples">Similar examples</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Zodiac_mosaics_in_ancient_synagogues&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5" title="Edit section: Similar examples"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwig"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaicootranto.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwiw"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaicootranto.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Mosaicootranto.jpg/250px-Mosaicootranto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Mosaicootranto.jpg/500px-Mosaicootranto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4928" data-file-height="3836" data-file-type="bitmap" height="195" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwjA"/></a><figcaption id="mwjQ">Examples appear in Christendom: here, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otranto_cathedral" title="Otranto cathedral" class="mw-redirect" id="mwjg">Otranto cathedral</a> </figcaption></figure>

<section data-mw-section-id="6" id="mwjw" aria-labelledby="Greece"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Greece">Greece</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Zodiac_mosaics_in_ancient_synagogues&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6" title="Edit section: Greece"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwkA">The combination of zodiac signs grouped around Helios and with personifications of the four seasons in the corners is typical for ancient synagogues from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eretz_Israel" title="Eretz Israel" class="mw-redirect" id="mwkQ">Eretz Israel</a>.<sup about="#mwt36" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-RJ01_12-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"RJ01"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-RJ01-12"}}'><a href="#cite_note-RJ01-12" id="mwkg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwkw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwlA">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwlQ">]</span></span></a></sup> However, Ruth Jacoby signalled in 2001 the only known exception, from the Tallaras Baths in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astypalaia" title="Astypalaia" id="mwlg">Astypalaea</a>, Greece.<sup about="#mwt37" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-RJ01_12-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"RJ01"}}'><a href="#cite_note-RJ01-12" id="mwlw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwmA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwmQ">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwmg">]</span></span></a></sup> There, the scheme is completed by the personifications of the twelve solar months, which surround those of the lunar calendar (the zodiac signs).<sup about="#mwt38" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-RJ01_12-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"RJ01"}}'><a href="#cite_note-RJ01-12" id="mwmw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwnA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwnQ">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwng">]</span></span></a></sup> Additionally, two more similar examples from Greece are known, none of them synagogues, where the four seasons in the corners are however replaced by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_winds" title="Four winds" class="mw-redirect" id="mwnw">four winds</a> of heaven: one in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sparta" title="Sparta" id="mwoA">Sparta</a> (4th century), which also has the solar calendar (the twelve months), and one from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thessaloniki" title="Thessaloniki" id="mwoQ">Thessaloniki</a>.<sup about="#mwt39" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-RJ01_12-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"RJ01"}}'><a href="#cite_note-RJ01-12" id="mwog"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwow"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwpA">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwpQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Jacoby suggests that the depiction of the lunar and solar calendars, both in use at the time, must have been such common motifs that they reached even such a remote place as the island of Astypalaea.<sup about="#mwt40" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-RJ01_12-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"RJ01"}}'><a href="#cite_note-RJ01-12" id="mwpg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwpw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqA">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="7" id="mwqg" aria-labelledby="Roman_zodiac_iconography"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Roman_zodiac_iconography">Roman zodiac iconography</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Zodiac_mosaics_in_ancient_synagogues&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7" title="Edit section: Roman zodiac iconography"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<section data-mw-section-id="8" id="mwqw" aria-labelledby="Zodiac_and_days_of_the_week_mosaic"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Zodiac_and_days_of_the_week_mosaic">Zodiac and days of the week mosaic</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Zodiac_mosaics_in_ancient_synagogues&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8" title="Edit section: Zodiac and days of the week mosaic"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwrA"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Catalogue_Alaoui_Jours_et_Zodiaque.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwrQ"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Catalogue_Alaoui_Jours_et_Zodiaque.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Catalogue_Alaoui_Jours_et_Zodiaque.jpg/250px-Catalogue_Alaoui_Jours_et_Zodiaque.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Catalogue_Alaoui_Jours_et_Zodiaque.jpg/500px-Catalogue_Alaoui_Jours_et_Zodiaque.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1800" data-file-height="2039" data-file-type="bitmap" height="283" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwrg"/></a><figcaption id="mwrw">Pagan 3rd-century Roman zodiac mosaic floor with hexagonal pattern. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bardo_National_Museum_(Tunis)" title="Bardo National Museum (Tunis)" id="mwsA">Bardo National Museum (Tunis)</a></figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwsQ">The mosaic reproduced here dates to the 3rd century,<sup about="#mwt41" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Ziqua_13-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Ziqua"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Ziqua-13"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Ziqua-13" id="mwsg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwsw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtA">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtQ">]</span></span></a></sup> the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_Roman" title="Imperial Roman" class="mw-redirect" id="mwtg">Imperial Roman</a> period. At the   centre it has the representations of the seven Roman gods after whom the days of the week were named in Latin – in the centre <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cronus" title="Cronus" id="mwtw">Cronus</a> or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saturn_(mythology)" title="Saturn (mythology)" id="mwuA">Saturn</a> (Saturday), then clockwise from top <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus_(mythology)" title="Venus (mythology)" id="mwuQ">Venus</a> (Friday), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mars_(mythology)" title="Mars (mythology)" id="mwug">Mars</a> (Tuesday), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luna_(goddess)" title="Luna (goddess)" id="mwuw">Luna</a> (Monday), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sol_(Roman_mythology)" title="Sol (Roman mythology)" id="mwvA">Sol</a> (Sunday), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercury_(mythology)" title="Mercury (mythology)" id="mwvQ">Mercury</a> (Wednesday), and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iovis" title="Iovis" class="mw-redirect" id="mwvg">Iovis</a> (Thursday) – surrounded by the zodiac signs – clockwise from the top: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquarius_(astrology)" title="Aquarius (astrology)" id="mwvw">Aquarius</a> is missing, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virgo_(astrology)" title="Virgo (astrology)" id="mwwA">Virgo</a> is missing, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libra_(astrology)" title="Libra (astrology)" id="mwwQ">Libra</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scorpio_(astrology)" title="Scorpio (astrology)" id="mwwg">Scorpio</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sagittarius_(astrology)" title="Sagittarius (astrology)" id="mwww">Sagittarius</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capricorn_(astrology)" title="Capricorn (astrology)" id="mwxA">Capricorn</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aries_(astrology)" title="Aries (astrology)" id="mwxQ">Aries</a> is missing, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pisces_(astrology)" title="Pisces (astrology)" id="mwxg">Pisces</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_(astrology)" title="Leo (astrology)" id="mwxw">Leo</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taurus_(astrology)" title="Taurus (astrology)" id="mwyA">Taurus</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemini_(astrology)" title="Gemini (astrology)" id="mwyQ">Gemini</a> is missing, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancer_(astrology)" title="Cancer (astrology)" id="mwyg">Cancer</a>.<sup about="#mwt42" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Ziqua_13-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Ziqua"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Ziqua-13" id="mwyw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwzA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwzQ">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwzg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt43" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ZaA_14-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ZaA"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-ZaA-14"}}'><a href="#cite_note-ZaA-14" id="mwzw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw0A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw0Q">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw0g">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt44" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-MCA_15-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"MCA"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-MCA-15"}}'><a href="#cite_note-MCA-15" id="mw0w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw1A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw1Q">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw1g">]</span></span></a></sup> The mosaic comes from Bir Chana in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaghouan_Governorate" title="Zaghouan Governorate" id="mw1w">Zaghouan region</a> of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunisia" title="Tunisia" id="mw2A">Tunisia</a> (part of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Africa_(Roman_province)" title="Africa (Roman province)" id="mw2Q">Roman province of Africa</a> at the time) and is on display at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bardo_National_Museum_(Tunis)" title="Bardo National Museum (Tunis)" id="mw2g">Bardo Museum in Tunis</a>.<sup about="#mwt45" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Ziqua_13-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Ziqua"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Ziqua-13" id="mw2w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw3A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw3Q">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw3g">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt46" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-MCA_15-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"MCA"}}'><a href="#cite_note-MCA-15" id="mw3w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw4A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4Q">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4g">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="9" id="mw4w" aria-labelledby="Mithraic_imagery"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Mithraic_imagery">Mithraic imagery</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Zodiac_mosaics_in_ancient_synagogues&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9" title="Edit section: Mithraic imagery"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mw5A">Roman <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mithraic" title="Mithraic" class="mw-redirect" id="mw5Q">Mithraic</a> depictions of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mithraism#Bull-slaying_scene" title="Mithraism" id="mw5g">Mithras slaying the bull</a> can contain the images of Sol in his <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadriga" title="Quadriga" id="mw5w">quadriga</a> and the zodiac signs.<sup about="#mwt49" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-griffith_16-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"griffith"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-griffith-16"}}'><a href="#cite_note-griffith-16" id="mw6A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw6Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw6g">[</span>16<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw6w">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="10" id="mw7A" aria-labelledby="See_also"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="See_also">See also</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Zodiac_mosaics_in_ancient_synagogues&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10" title="Edit section: See also"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<ul id="mw7Q"><li id="mw7g"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_synagogues_in_Palestine" title="Ancient synagogues in Palestine" id="mw7w">Ancient synagogues in Palestine</a></li>
<li id="mw8A"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Jewish_art" title="Ancient Jewish art" id="mw8Q">Ancient Jewish art</a></li>
<li id="mw8g"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dendera_temple_complex" title="Dendera temple complex" id="mw8w">Dendera temple complex</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dendera_zodiac" title="Dendera zodiac" id="mw9A">zodiac</a> (Egypt)</li>
<li id="mw9Q"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gezer_calendar" title="Gezer calendar" id="mw9g">Gezer calendar</a></li>
<li id="mw9w"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jewish_views_on_astrology" title="Jewish views on astrology" id="mw-A">Jewish views on astrology</a></li>
<li id="mw-Q"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historic_synagogues" title="Historic synagogues" id="mw-g">Historic synagogues</a>
